# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Section 6 (Conceptual Framework)

REVS = {
    "P329": ("This section converts the propositions into a working model.",
             "This section converts the propositions into a working model called SETILL: a Systemic Living Lab for Just Energy Transition. It has ten components, each naming something the lab has to organise, decide or measure. The components are not stages; they run in parallel and feed one another, and the numbering is a convenience for reference. The distinction between the sections is that between a theoretical and a conceptual framework: Section 5 justifies the ontology, this section builds the model it implies, with enough specificity that another researcher could apply it and enough openness that the case can surprise it. A model that cannot be surprised is not a research instrument."),
    "P332": ("The framework is a structured account",
             "The framework is a structured account of the elements that must be aligned for a community-scale energy innovation to be taken up, sustained and judged fair in an informal settlement. It is used in three ways: as a design heuristic for the project team, as an analytical frame for the data, and as an evaluation instrument."),
    "P333": ("It is not a theory of everything.",
             "It is not a theory of everything. It does not explain national energy politics, model the electricity market or predict adoption rates. It is deliberately local, because the local configuration is where the fate of a decentralised technology is decided."),
    "P334": ("It is also not a checklist to be completed mechanically.",
             "It is also not a checklist to be completed mechanically. Each component raises a question that has to be answered in the specific case, and the answers will differ between settlements with different markets, space and organised structures. The framework's contribution is the set of questions, not a set of answers. Three commitments from Section 5 shape every component: the unit of analysis is the configuration, not the artefact and not the individual; justice is assessed throughout, not added at the end; and the lab is part of what it studies, so its own operation has to be documented."),
    "P337": ("Figure 4.1 presents the framework as a whole.",
             "Figure 4.1 presents the framework as a whole. It reads from left to right: the stakeholder set on the left is the group of actors whose relations constitute the lab; the four central blocks are the cycles of co-diagnosis, co-design, prototyping and evaluation; the right-hand blocks are the outputs \u2014 the technology artefact, the account of acceptance and the outcomes for households. The band at the bottom carries the justice criteria and governance rules, and the dashed arrow is the barrier pathway that can interrupt any cycle."),
    "P338": ("The diagram is deliberately drawn with feedback loops",
             "The diagram is deliberately drawn with feedback loops rather than arrows that only move forward. The loop marked R1 shows use feeding back into design; a second loop runs between evaluation and the stakeholder set, because the evaluation can change who is in the room. The framework expects this, and the lab has to accommodate it."),
    "P341": ("Figure 4.2 presents the justice dimension separately,",
             "Figure 4.2 presents the justice dimension separately, because it is the part of the framework most easily reduced to a slogan. Each of the four dimensions is shown with the question it asks and the evidence that answers it. The dimensions are not additive: a project can distribute benefits fairly and still exclude people from decisions, or include them and still fail to recognise their knowledge. The framework therefore reports a profile across the four dimensions rather than a single score."),
    "P345": ("Table 4.1 shows where each component comes from,",
             "Table 4.1 shows where each component comes from and Table 4.2 what each requires in practice. The paragraphs that follow explain the reasoning. The order groups the components into three clusters: establishing the frame (C1\u2013C3), organising the intervention (C4\u2013C7), and assessing and sustaining it (C8\u2013C10)."),
    "P351": ("Every project draws a boundary,",
             "Every project draws a boundary, and the boundary decides who is consulted, whose costs are counted and which infrastructure is treated as fixed. In most projects the boundary is drawn by convention and therefore cannot be questioned. This component makes it an explicit decision: the lab produces a dated boundary statement naming who is included, who is excluded and why, noting the actors who could affect the outcome but are not in the room \u2014 the landlord who controls the meter, the councillor who controls access, the department that owns the waste route, the supplier who stocks the part. The statement is revisited each cycle, because the boundary that made sense at the start may not make sense once fieldwork begins."),
    "P353": ("The component derives from Checkland's (1981) argument",
             "The component derives from Checkland's (1981) argument that a system is a way of organising inquiry rather than an object in the world, and from Midgley's (2000) boundary critique, which asks whose interests a framing serves; Arnstein (1969) informs the link between boundary and power. The test is simple: can the team produce a dated boundary statement with reasons, and show that it changed at least once in response to something learned in the field? If not, the boundary was probably inherited rather than chosen."),
    "P356": ("A diagnosis is an account of what the problem is,",
             "A diagnosis is an account of what the problem is, and in energy projects it is usually technical: the settlement lacks a reliable cooking fuel, the waste is not collected, the grid connection is inadequate. Such statements may be true, but they are made from a position. A woman cooking for eight, a tenant whose rent includes electricity, a landlord, an artisan and a municipal official will describe the same settlement differently. This component treats those differences as data: the lab collects accounts from multiple positions and uses them together, rather than selecting one as the accurate description. The methods capture practice and embodiment as well as opinion: transect walks, household diaries, photovoice, kitchen observation and key-informant interviews."),
    "P358": ("The component draws on Haraway's (1988) argument",
             "The component draws on Haraway's (1988) argument that knowledge is always situated, and on the South African literature showing that household energy practices are organised by gender, so that a diagnosis which ignores this mis-specifies the problem (Musango et al., 2020; Anditi et al., 2022; Musango, 2022). The test is whether the diagnosis contains accounts that differ by position, and whether those differences changed something in the design. A diagnosis that produces a single coherent story is suspicious; settlements are not coherent."),
    "P361": ("Participation is often organised as an event:",
             "Participation is often organised as an event: a meeting is announced, people attend, and attendance is recorded as participation. That measures presence, not influence, and it excludes those whose time is not their own. This component treats participation as a design problem: what would have to be true for a tenant who works all day, a woman with small children, a person with a disability or an elderly resident to take part on equal terms? The answers are practical: meeting times that fit the working day, venues within walking distance, childcare, translation into the languages people actually use, separate sessions where mixed groups would silence some voices, and payment for time where the project can afford it."),
    "P363": ("The component derives from Arnstein's (1969) ladder,",
             "The component derives from Arnstein's (1969) distinction between consultation, partnership and citizen control, and from the co-design literature, which treats users as participants in generating ideas rather than sources of requirements (Sanders and Stappers, 2008; Leminen et al., 2012). The test is whether attendance is broad and stable across the cycles, and whether the people who attend can change decisions. A participation architecture that produces good attendance and no change has failed."),
    "P366": ("The fourth component is the one that most directly distinguishes SETILL",
             "The fourth component most directly distinguishes SETILL from a conventional Living Lab. There the artefact is the object of design and the context the setting; here the configuration is the object of design, and the process must specify the other elements \u2014 fuel supply, water source, space, maintenance arrangement, spare-parts route, skills, tariff and rules of use. For biogas this means the specification includes the feedstock supply chain, the water budget, siting, gas storage, stove type, digestate disposal, the maintenance schedule and the person responsible for each task. A specification covering only digester volume and gas yield is incomplete, however technically sound."),
    "P368": ("The component rests on Barad's (2007) argument",
             "The component rests on Barad's (2007) argument that properties emerge through intra-action, on Bennett's (2010) account of thing-power, and on practice theory, which shows that technologies are enrolled in existing routines (Shove, 2003; Stephenson et al., 2010). The test is whether the specification names the non-technical requirements explicitly, and whether the prototype is a prototype of the arrangement or only of the vessel."),
    "P371": ("A community-scale system needs a decision-making structure.",
             "A community-scale system needs a decision-making structure. Who sits on the management committee, how are members chosen and removed, who owns the asset if the funder withdraws, who authorises and pays for a repair, who resolves a dispute over access? These questions are usually answered by default at the end of a project: the asset is registered to an organisation that may not function, the committee is whoever attended the final meeting, and the dispute route is a phone number that stops working when the project officer leaves. This component decides them explicitly and early, producing a written agreement in the local language naming the owner, the managing body, the decision rules, the tariff principle and the dispute route \u2014 a document residents can describe and hold each other to."),
    "P374": ("The component draws on the commons literature",
             "The component draws on the commons literature, particularly Ostrom's (1990) principle that boundaries, rules and conflict-resolution mechanisms should be defined by the users themselves, and on von Wirth et al. (2019), who show that a lab's durability depends on its embedding in local institutions. The test is whether residents can describe, in their own words, who owns the system and what happens when it breaks. If the answer is \u201cthe project\u201d, the component is incomplete."),
    "P377": ("Every intervention distributes cost.",
             "Every intervention distributes cost. Some is financial: capital, operating expenses, fuel, spare parts. Some is labour: collecting feedstock, carrying water, cleaning the digester, attending meetings. Some is risk: the possibility that the system fails and the household falls back on a costlier option. This component traces the distribution \u2014 who pays, in what form, at which point in the service chain \u2014 and disaggregates by tenure, income and gender, because the same nominal cost can be trivial for an owner and decisive for a tenant. It draws on the energy justice literature on affordability and the distribution of burdens (Sovacool and Dworkin, 2015; Walker and Day, 2012), and on the argument that unpaid labour is a cost even when no money changes hands."),
    "P380": ("The test is a cost profile by household type",
             "The test is a cost profile by household type that includes unpaid labour. A project reporting a low cost per household without disaggregating has not answered the question."),
    "P382": ("A Living Lab that leaves behind a working system",
             "A Living Lab that leaves a working system but no capacity to maintain it has not succeeded. This component treats capability as an outcome in its own right: skills in construction, operation, maintenance and repair; a local supplier of spare parts; a small enterprise able to service other installations; people who can train others. It is informed by Braidotti's (2013) distinction between potestas and potentia, framing capacity-building as an increase in the community's power to act, and is compatible with the capability approach (Nussbaum, 2011). The test is whether the skills persist after the project team leaves; a workshop that produces certificates but no practice has not built capability."),
    "P386": ("This component is the operational heart",
             "This component is the operational heart of the framework's normative claim. It turns each justice dimension into indicators with a data source and a point of comparison, set out in Table 4.4 and discussed in Section 6.6. It is not a scoring exercise: it produces a profile showing where the arrangement performs well and where it does not, shared with participants before it is finalised, with disagreements recorded."),
    "P389": ("A Living Lab is an apparatus in Barad's (2007) sense:",
             "A Living Lab is an apparatus in Barad's (2007) sense: it participates in producing the phenomenon it studies, and its questions, participants and records enact a cut. This component makes the cut visible through three records. A decision log: what was decided, by whom, on what basis, and which alternatives were rejected. An intervention register: every action that could have affected the outcome, from installing the digester to choosing a meeting venue. And a reflection record: the research team's notes on its own assumptions, surprises and discomforts."),
    "P391": ("These records serve two purposes.",
             "These records allow the analysis to distinguish project effects from other changes in the settlement, and allow the reader to assess the lab's own role in what it found. Without them, a finding about residents' preferences may be a finding about the questions the project asked."),
    "P393": ("The final component plans for the end from the beginning.",
             "The final component plans for the end from the beginning. It asks what will still be working two years after the project closes, who will maintain it, how it will be financed, and what happens to the relationships. The exit plan specifies the handover (asset, records, spare parts, training materials, contacts), the finance (a maintenance fund, a tariff, a municipal budget line or a combination), and the review: a date at which the arrangement is assessed by the people who depend on it."),
    "P395": ("The component draws on von Wirth et al. (2019),",
             "The component draws on von Wirth et al. (2019), who show that a lab's impact depends on how it is embedded in wider structures, and on Mbatha and Musango's (2022) observation that many energy Living Labs in Africa are not designed for continuation. The test is a named owner, a budget line and a maintenance route that do not depend on the research team."),
    "P398": ("The components are not independent,",
             "The components are not independent, and four relationships matter. A dependency: C4 depends on C2, because a configuration cannot be designed without a diagnosis of what it currently is \u2014 yet projects routinely design technology before diagnosing the arrangement, and discover during implementation that water, space or maintenance were never assessed. A tension: C3 and C10 pull against each other, because broad participation takes time and later cycles are under pressure to close; the framework requires the lab to record how the tension was managed. A feedback: C8 feeds C4, because the justice evaluation produces findings that should change the design \u2014 if tenants bear a disproportionate cost, the tariff or the siting must respond. A precondition: C1 and C5 make the other components possible, since a lab without a boundary statement does not know what it is assessing, and one without a governance agreement cannot hand anything over."),
    "P403": ("Table 4.3 maps the research questions onto the components",
             "Table 4.3 maps the research questions onto the components and the evidence that will answer them. No single question is answered by a single component: RQ2, for example, is answered mainly through C2 and C3, but also through C6, because cost is a condition of participation."),
    "P407": ("The justice dimension needs more detail than the other components,",
             "The justice dimension needs more detail than the others, because it is the one most easily reduced to rhetoric. Table 4.4 sets out the criteria, indicators and data sources. The indicators are deliberately modest: each can be observed or asked about, and each can produce an unfavourable answer."),
    "P410": ("Two features of the indicator set deserve comment.",
             "Two features deserve comment. The indicators are mostly relational rather than perceptual: \u201cdecisions changed as a result of resident input\u201d requires a record of both the decision and the input, whereas \u201csatisfaction with participation\u201d can be high in a process that changed nothing; the framework prefers the former while collecting both. And the indicators are designed to allow a mixed profile, which is the point: a project may show good distribution, weak procedural influence and strong recognition, and the report will say so. A framework that produces a single number invites the reader to ignore what the number conceals."),
    "P413": ("The framework also applies the justice criteria to the lab itself.",
             "The framework also applies the justice criteria to the lab itself: it consumes time, attention and space, decides who is included, and distributes the benefits of participation unevenly. C9 exists to make this visible, and the evaluation includes a short assessment of the lab's own performance."),
    "P415": ("The framework does three jobs in the analysis,",
             "The framework does three jobs in the analysis; stating them guards against the framework becoming a set of headings under which anything can be filed. The first is deductive: the ten components provide an initial coding frame for interview and observational data \u2014 a passage about the timing of meetings is coded to C3, one about ownership of the digester to C5 \u2014 which gives the analysis a starting structure and comparability across sources."),
    "P417": ("The second job is abductive.",
             "The second is abductive. The framework is expected to be incomplete, and where the data do not fit a component, or a component proves unimportant, that is a finding. The analysis records the misfits and uses them to revise the model \u2014 the moment at which the study can contribute to theory rather than merely apply it (Alvesson and K\u00e4rreman, 2007)."),
    "P418": ("The third job is evaluative.",
             "The third is evaluative. The justice criteria are applied at baseline and follow-up, and the profile is one of the main outputs: a description of how a particular arrangement distributed benefits, voice, recognition and repair at a particular time, not a verdict on the community or the team. The risk \u2014 an evaluation conducted by the project team about its own work \u2014 is managed in three ways: criteria specified in advance, data that the team did not generate (diaries, observation by an assistant not involved in delivery), and discussion of the draft profile with participants before it is finalised."),
    "P421": ("The framework has four limits that should be stated.",
             "The framework has four limits. It is designed for community-scale, decentralised technology in a low-income urban settlement and may not transfer to household devices, networked infrastructure or rural settings without modification. It assumes a research team present in the field over an extended period; a three-month consultancy could use it as a checklist but could not complete the cycles or observe durability. It depends on access: a lab that cannot reach tenants will produce a partial picture, and the boundary statement should say so. And it is a framework for design and evaluation, not causal inference: it can show that an arrangement changed and describe how, but cannot isolate the lab's contribution from other changes. Section 7 addresses this through case logic and the reflexive record, not statistical control."),
    "P427": ("This section has converted the theoretical propositions",
             "This section has converted the theoretical propositions of Section 5 into a ten-component model covering the frame (boundary, diagnosis, participation), the intervention (configuration, governance, cost, capability) and the assessment (justice, reflexivity, exit), each with a core question, activities and a test. The justice dimension is operationalised in twelve indicators with data sources. The framework is offered as an instrument that can be used, criticised and revised; its value will be judged by whether it produces findings a conventional technology-transfer approach would have missed, and by whether those findings are useful to the people who live in the settlement. That judgement can only be made after the fieldwork, and the design in Section 7 is intended to make it possible."),
}
